\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\ul\b\f0\fs32\lang9\u-10180?\u-8385?\f1  SAFE STEPS PARENT WORKBOOK\par

\pard\sl240\slmult1\ulnone\b0\f0\fs22\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 1 \f2\emdash\f1  What Children Need to Feel Safe\par
Key Message\par
Children grow best when they feel safe, seen, and supported.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They need emotional safety, not just physical safety.\par
\par
They need to know you\rquote re present and predictable.\par
\par
They need warmth, connection, and calm.\par
\par
Safety helps their brain learn and grow.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Offer warm eye contact.\par
\par
Use gentle touch (if your child is comfortable).\par
\par
Keep your voice soft and steady.\par
\par
Create a simple daily routine.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What helps my child feel safe with me?\par
\par
What moments of connection can I create each day?\par
\par
How do I show my child they matter?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 2 \f2\emdash\f1  Warmth + Boundaries: The Parenting Balance\par
Key Message\par
Children need both love and limits.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Warmth helps them feel loved.\par
\par
Boundaries help them feel safe.\par
\par
Too much freedom feels scary.\par
\par
Too many rules feels harsh.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Set simple, clear rules.\par
\par
Follow through calmly.\par
\par
Praise positive behaviour.\par
\par
Use warmth even when correcting.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
Do I lean more toward warmth or boundaries?\par
\par
What boundary does my child need most right now?\par
\par
How can I stay calm when enforcing limits?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 3 \f2\emdash\f1  Emotional Coaching Basics\par
Key Message\par
Children learn emotions from us.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They need help naming feelings.\par
\par
They need your calm presence during big emotions.\par
\par
They learn emotional skills through modelling.\par
\par
Emotional coaching builds resilience.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Name feelings: \ldblquote You\rquote re frustrated.\rdblquote\par
\par
Validate: \ldblquote That makes sense.\rdblquote\par
\par
Guide: \ldblquote Let\rquote s breathe together.\rdblquote\par
\par
Teach after calm: \ldblquote Next time, try\'85\rdblquote\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I respond to my child\rquote s big feelings?\par
\par
What emotions are hardest for me to handle?\par
\par
How can I model healthy emotional expression?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 4 \f2\emdash\f1  Understanding Your Child\rquote s Needs\par
Key Message\par
Behaviour shows needs.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They may not have the words to express needs.\par
\par
Behaviour is communication.\par
\par
Needs may include hunger, tiredness, fear, overwhelm, or connection.\par
\par
Meeting needs reduces behaviour challenges.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Pause before reacting.\par
\par
Ask: \ldblquote What does my child need right now?\rdblquote\par
\par
Offer connection first.\par
\par
Keep snacks, water, and comfort items available.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What behaviours confuse me most?\par
\par
What needs might be underneath?\par
\par
How can I respond with curiosity?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 5 \f2\emdash\f1  Positive Discipline (Without Punishment)\par
Key Message\par
Discipline means teaching, not punishing.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Punishment creates fear, not learning.\par
\par
Positive discipline teaches skills.\par
\par
Children need guidance, not shame.\par
\par
Calm correction works better than yelling.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Use natural consequences.\par
\par
Teach the skill they\rquote re missing.\par
\par
Model the behaviour you want.\par
\par
Praise effort, not perfection.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What discipline strategies do I use most?\par
\par
Do they teach or punish?\par
\par
What skill does my child need help learning?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 6 \f2\emdash\f1  Daily Routines That Work\par
Key Message\par
Predictability builds safety.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Routines reduce anxiety.\par
\par
They help children know what to expect.\par
\par
They make transitions easier.\par
\par
They support emotional regulation.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Create a simple morning routine.\par
\par
Use a visual schedule.\par
\par
Give 2\f3\_\f1 minute warnings before transitions.\par
\par
Keep bedtime consistent.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What routines help my child feel calm?\par
\par
Where do we need more predictability?\par
\par
What small routine can I add this week?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 7 \f2\emdash\f1  Repair After Conflict\par
Key Message\par
Repair matters more than perfection.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Conflict is normal.\par
\par
Apologising builds trust.\par
\par
Repair teaches emotional safety.\par
\par
Children learn from your example.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Say: \ldblquote I\rquote m sorry. I shouldn\rquote t have yelled.\rdblquote\par
\par
Reconnect with a hug or gentle touch.\par
\par
Talk about feelings after everyone is calm.\par
\par
Model healthy repair.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I repair after conflict?\par
\par
What helps my child reconnect?\par
\par
What can I do differently next time?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 8 \f2\emdash\f1  Co\f3\_\f1 Regulation for Everyday Parenting\par
Key Message\par
Children borrow your calm.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They cannot calm down alone.\par
\par
Your calm helps their brain settle.\par
\par
Co\f3\_\f1 regulation builds self\f3\_\f1 regulation.\par
\par
Connection comes before correction.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Take a slow breath before responding.\par
\par
Sit beside your child.\par
\par
Use simple phrases: \ldblquote I\rquote m here. You\rquote re safe.\rdblquote\par
\par
Teach after calm.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I feel during my child\rquote s meltdowns?\par
\par
What helps me stay calm?\par
\par
What co\f3\_\f1 regulation strategies can I try?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 9 \f2\emdash\f1  Building Connection Through Play\par
Key Message\par
Play is connection.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Play strengthens your bond.\par
\par
It builds trust and safety.\par
\par
It helps children express emotions.\par
\par
Even 10 minutes a day makes a difference.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Let your child lead the play.\par
\par
Follow their ideas.\par
\par
Put your phone away.\par
\par
Use play to reconnect after conflict.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What play does my child enjoy most?\par
\par
How can I make time for connection?\par
\par
What moments of joy can we create?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 10 \f2\emdash\f1  Communication That Builds Trust\par
Key Message\par
How you speak shapes how your child feels.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Calm communication builds trust.\par
\par
Clear instructions reduce stress.\par
\par
Tone matters more than words.\par
\par
Children listen better when they feel safe.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Use short, simple sentences.\par
\par
Get down to their eye level.\par
\par
Speak softly and slowly.\par
\par
Praise cooperation.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
How do I communicate when stressed?\par
\par
How does my child respond to my tone?\par
\par
What communication habit can I improve?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 11 \f2\emdash\f1  Managing Stress as a Parent\par
Key Message\par
Your calm is your child\rquote s calm.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
They feel your stress.\par
\par
They need your emotional stability.\par
\par
Self\f3\_\f1 care is not selfish \f2\emdash\f1  it\rquote s necessary.\par
\par
Small resets help you stay grounded.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Take 3 slow breaths before responding.\par
\par
Step outside for 1 minute of fresh air.\par
\par
Drink water regularly.\par
\par
Ask for help when needed.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What stresses me most?\par
\par
How does my stress affect my child?\par
\par
What small reset can I use daily?\par
\par
Notes\par
(Blank space)\par
\par
\f0\u-10179?\u-9000?\f1  EPISODE 12 \f2\emdash\f1  Creating a Safe, Predictable Home\par
Key Message\par
Safety is built daily.\par
\par
What This Means for Your Child\par
Small moments matter.\par
\par
Predictability reduces anxiety.\par
\par
Warmth builds trust.\par
\par
Consistency creates long\f3\_\f1 term safety.\par
\par
Try This at Home\par
Keep routines steady.\par
\par
Use connection rituals (morning hug, bedtime story).\par
\par
Offer choices to build confidence.\par
\par
Celebrate small wins.\par
\par
Reflection Questions\par
What makes our home feel safe?\par
\par
What routines help us stay connected?\par
\par
What can I do today to build safety?\par
\par
